abstract:
Observations of neutral-current $\ensuremath{
u}$ interactions on deuterium in the Sudbury Neutrino Observatory are reported. Using the neutral current (NC), elastic scattering, and charged current reactions and assuming the standard ${}^{8}\mathrm{B}$ shape, the ${\ensuremath{
u}}_{e}$ component of the ${}^{8}\mathrm{B}$ solar flux is ${\ensuremath{\varphi}}_{e}{\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}=\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}1.76}_{\ensuremath{-}0.05}^{+0.05}(\mathrm{stat}{)}_{\ensuremath{-}0.09}^{+0.09}(\mathrm{syst})\ifmmode	imes\else	exttimes i{}{10}^{6}\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}{\mathrm{cm}}^{\ensuremath{-}2}{\mathrm{s}}^{\ensuremath{-}1}$ for a kinetic energy threshold of 5 MeV. The non- ${\ensuremath{
u}}_{e}$ component is ${\ensuremath{\varphi}}_{\ensuremath{\mu}\ensuremath{	au}}{\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}=\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}3.41}_{\ensuremath{-}0.45}^{+0.45}(\mathrm{stat}{)}_{\ensuremath{-}0.45}^{+0.48}(\mathrm{syst})\ifmmode	imes\else	exttimes i{}{10}^{6}\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}{\mathrm{cm}}^{\ensuremath{-}2}{\mathrm{s}}^{\ensuremath{-}1}$, $5.3\ensuremath{\sigma}$ greater than zero, providing strong evidence for solar ${\ensuremath{
u}}_{e}$ flavor transformation. The total flux measured with the NC reaction is ${\ensuremath{\varphi}}_{\mathrm{NC}}{\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}=\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}5.09}_{\ensuremath{-}0.43}^{+0.44}(\mathrm{stat}{)}_{\ensuremath{-}0.43}^{+0.46}(\mathrm{syst})\ifmmode	imes\else	exttimes i{}{10}^{6}\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}{\mathrm{cm}}^{\ensuremath{-}2}{\mathrm{s}}^{\ensuremath{-}1}$, consistent with solar models.

KamLAND has measured the flux of ${\overline{\ensuremath{
u}}}_{e}$'s from distant nuclear reactors. We find fewer ${\overline{\ensuremath{
u}}}_{e}$ events than expected from standard assumptions about ${\overline{\ensuremath{
u}}}_{e}$ propagation at the 99.95% C.L. In a $162	ext{ }\mathrm{	ext{ton}}\ifmmode\cdot\else	extperiodcentered i{}\mathrm{y}\mathrm{r}$ exposure the ratio of the observed inverse $\ensuremath{ eta}$-decay events to the expected number without ${\overline{\ensuremath{
u}}}_{e}$ disappearance is $0.611\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}0.085\mathrm{(}\mathrm{s}\mathrm{t}\mathrm{a}\mathrm{t}\mathrm{)}\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}0.041\mathrm{(}\mathrm{s}\mathrm{y}\mathrm{s}\mathrm{t}\mathrm{)}$ for ${\overline{\ensuremath{
u}}}_{e}$ energies $>3.4	ext{ }	ext{ }\mathrm{M}\mathrm{e}\mathrm{V}$. In the context of two-flavor neutrino oscillations with CPT invariance, all solutions to the solar neutrino problem except for the ``large mixing angle'' region are excluded.

Solar neutrinos from ${}^{8}\mathrm{B}$ decay have been detected at the Sudbury Neutrino Observatory via the charged current (CC) reaction on deuterium and the elastic scattering (ES) of electrons. The flux of ${\ensuremath{
u}}_{e}$'s is measured by the CC reaction rate to be ${\ensuremath{\varphi}}^{\mathrm{CC}}({\ensuremath{
u}}_{e})\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}=\phantom{ule{0ex}{0ex}}1.75\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}0.07(\mathrm{stat}{)}_{\ensuremath{-}0.11}^{+0.12}(\mathrm{syst})\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}0.05(\mathrm{theor})\ifmmode	imes\else	exttimes i{}{10}^{6}{\mathrm{cm}}^{\ensuremath{-}2}{\mathrm{s}}^{\ensuremath{-}1}$. Comparison of ${\ensuremath{\varphi}}^{\mathrm{CC}}({\ensuremath{
u}}_{e})$ to the Super-Kamiokande Collaboration's precision value of the flux inferred from the ES reaction yields a $3.3\ensuremath{\sigma}$ difference, assuming the systematic uncertainties are normally distributed, providing evidence of an active non- ${\ensuremath{
u}}_{e}$ component in the solar flux. The total flux of active ${}^{8}\mathrm{B}$ neutrinos is determined to be $5.44\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}0.99\ifmmode	imes\else	exttimes i{}{10}^{6}{\mathrm{cm}}^{\ensuremath{-}2}{\mathrm{s}}^{\ensuremath{-}1}$.
